To find the modular inverse of $11$ modulo $1000$, we need to find an integer $x$ such that $11x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}$.

We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the modular inverse.

\begin{align*}
1000 &= 11 \cdot 90 + 10 \\
11 &= 10 \cdot 1 + 1
\end{align*}

Working backwards, we have:

\begin{align*}
1 &= 11 - 10 \cdot 1 \\
&= 11 - (1000 - 11 \cdot 90) \cdot 1 \\
&= 11 \cdot 91 - 1000 \cdot 1
\end{align*}

Therefore, the modular inverse of $11$ modulo $1000$ is $\boxed{91}$.The answer is: 91

