solution_steps:
To find the base $2$ representation of $84_{10}$, we divide $84$ by $2$ repeatedly until the quotient is $0$.
The remainders at each step will give us the base $2$ digits in reverse order.
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1010100_2} \\
\cline{2-2}
2 & 84 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{42} \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{21} \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{10} \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{5} \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{2} \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1} \\
\end{array}
\]
So the base $2$ representation of $84_{10}$ is $\boxed{1010100_2}$.
The answer is: 1010100_2

We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the two given numbers.
First, we divide $n^3 + 3^2$ by $n + 2$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{n^2 - 2n + 4} \\
\cline{2-3}
n + 2 & n^3 + 3^2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{n^3 + 2n^2} \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-2n^2 + 9} \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-2n^2 - 4n} \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{13n + 9} \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{13n + 26} \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-17}
\end{array}
\]
Since the remainder is $-17$, we divide $n + 2$ by $-17$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-1} \\
\cline{2-2}
-17 & n + 2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-17n - 34} \\
\cline{2-2}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{36}
\end{array}
\]
The remainder is $36$, so the GCD of $n^3 + 3^2$ and $n + 2$ is the GCD of $-17$ and $36$.
The prime factorization of $-17$ is $-1 \cdot 17$ and the prime factorization of $36$ is $2^2 \cdot 3^2$.
The only common prime factor of $-17$ and $36$ is $1$, so the GCD is $1$.
Therefore, the greatest common divisor of $n^3 + 3^2$ and $n + 2$ is $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

